; BIG TIMES BIG ON THE FIXED-LIMB RUNG, and a big LITERAL to make it.
;
; Two things neither `big-limbs` nor `big-select` exercised, and the gap hid a
; WRONG ANSWER: `mul`'s schoolbook row clamped its store index when `i+j`
; reached the declared width, so a partial product past the last limb was
; written into slot 0 and the answer came back wrong in its low digits. A clamp
; says "do something safe" and the only safe thing at a declared width is to
; FAIL. It now checks the contribution and the carry and traps.
;
; The literal is 30 digits, which is past `int64` and so past what `KInt` can
; hold. `core/read.go` desugars it into Horner over base 10^15, every leaf
; inside the portable window and every operator the language's own — the same
; move `(pow 2 70)` made for range endpoints, one level down.
;
; inputs: 0 1 7 1000
; expect: 15241578753238836750495351562536198787501905199875019052100 15241578753238836750495351562659655576514250878776253619990 15241578753238836750495351563400396310588324952183661027330 15241578753238836750495351685992987799847584101109586942100
;
; Computed independently rather than by a second implementation of ours.
;
; `; skip: windows` -- the arithmetic works there; rendering a limb table as
; decimal needs a string surface this language does not have. See `big-select`.
; skip: windows
(sig sq ((n (int 0 1000))) (int 0 (pow 2 400)))

(def sq (fn (n)
  (* 123456789012345678901234567890 (+ 123456789012345678901234567890 n))))

(def run (fn (n) (big-str (sq n))))
